# Architecture — how this app rides the platform

This repo contributes **one namespace** of workloads to a shared k3s cluster.
The platform (Traefik, cert-manager, external-dns, External Secrets Operator,
Prometheus/Loki, MetalLB) is shared; this repo supplies the app and its
per-namespace custom resources. Flux reconciles `kubernetes/flux/` into
`{{ app_namespace }}`.

## Request path

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  user(["User"]) -->|"{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}"| edge["Public DNS + edge"]
  edge --> lb["MetalLB VIP"]
  lb --> traefik["Traefik IngressRoute<br/>+ hsts-header{% if enable_sso %}<br/>+ authentik-auth{% endif %}"]
  traefik --> svc["Service :{{ app_port }}"]
  svc --> pod["App pod<br/>non-root, read-only rootfs"]
```

- **Public** `{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}`: the `IngressRoute` carries
  `external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io/target: {{ external_domain }}`, so
  external-dns creates the record automatically. No operator action.
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}
- **Internal** `{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}`: reached only on the
  LAN/tailnet via the `lan-tailscale-only` middleware. The internal name is
  **not** auto-provisioned — the operator adds the record (ONBOARDING step O5).
{%- else %}
- **Internal**: not published. `enable_internal_ingress` renders a second
  IngressRoute and its Certificate, plus one operator DNS step.
{%- endif %}
- **TLS**: a per-host `Certificate` (ClusterIssuer `letsencrypt-prod`, DNS-01)
  issues a Secret into this namespace, and Traefik terminates from it. Per-host
  rather than wildcard: Let's Encrypt caps duplicate certificates at 5 per week
  and the platform already issues the shared wildcards.
- **Middlewares** live in the platform `traefik` namespace and are referenced
  cross-namespace; the cluster enables `allowCrossNamespace`.

## Secret flow
{% if secrets_backend == 'none' %}
This app declares no secrets. When it grows one, the shape is: an
`ExternalSecret` in this repo referencing the operator-owned
`ClusterSecretStore`, which pulls the value into a Kubernetes Secret that the
Deployment binds with `secretKeyRef`. Values never enter git.
{%- else %}
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  backend["{{ 'Vault items, prefixed with the slug' if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' else 'GitLab CI/CD variables' }}"]
  backend --> css["ClusterSecretStore<br/>{{ secrets_backend }}-{{ app_slug }}<br/>(operator-owned, namespace-scoped)"]
  css --> es["ExternalSecret<br/>(this repo)"]
  es --> sec[("Kubernetes Secret")]
  sec -->|secretKeyRef| pod["App pod"]
```

- Secret **values** never enter git. This repo holds only the `ExternalSecret`.
- The store is scoped to `{{ app_namespace }}` with `spec.conditions` — without
  it a `ClusterSecretStore` is readable from every namespace in the cluster.
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
- Items are prefixed with the slug in the shared vault
  (`remoteRef.key: "{{ app_slug }}: <Item>"`), which isolates them from other
  tenants' items.
{%- endif %}
- Keys are hyphenated, so the Deployment binds each one with `secretKeyRef`; an
  `envFrom: secretRef` would silently skip them (hyphens are not valid env-var
  names).
{%- endif %}

## Storage & scheduling

The app is **stateless** as generated, and soft-avoids the storage node
(`{{ node_label_domain }}/nas`). Persistent, zvol-backed data is an
operator-assisted step: the operator provisions the volume and a matching PV,
and the pod is pinned to the storage node with a required hostname affinity plus
a toleration for `{{ node_label_domain }}/nas=true:PreferNoSchedule`. NFS-backed
volumes mount **by hostname** (a wildcard certificate carries no IP SAN, so an
IP mount fails the TLS handshake). See ONBOARDING step O6 — the details are
cluster-specific and this template ships no PVC.

## Network policy

`networkpolicy.yaml` denies all ingress and egress, then opens Traefik to the
app port{% if enable_servicemonitor %}, Prometheus to the metrics port{% endif %}, cluster DNS, and public HTTPS. The public rule's
`except:` list is the RFC-reserved and private IPv4 space — a standards
constant, not this cluster's addressing — so the app cannot reach LAN, tailnet,
loopback or cloud-metadata addresses.

Anything else needs its own egress rule. Three worked examples:

```yaml
# Other pods in THIS namespace (a database or cache beside the app). The pod
# and Service CIDRs sit inside the 10.0.0.0/8 except above, so same-namespace
# traffic is denied until this rule exists.
- to:
    - podSelector: {}

# SMTP relay (submission).
- to: [{ipBlock: {cidr: <relay-ip>/32}}]
  ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 587}]

# kube-apiserver. kube-proxy DNATs the in-cluster VIP before NetworkPolicy is
# evaluated, so the SERVER NODE IPs are the allowlist — the ClusterIP never
# matches.
- to:
    - {ipBlock: {cidr: <server-node-ip>/32}}
  ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 6443}]
```

## Autoscaling & resilience

{% if enable_hpa -%}
- **HPA** owns the replica count on CPU (`minReplicas: 2`), so the Deployment
  ships no `replicas:` and the **VPA** is memory-only — two autoscalers on one
  resource fight.
{%- else -%}
- **VPA** (`updateMode: Initial`) right-sizes the pod on natural restarts,
  without evicting a running one. Set `updateMode: "Off"` for a stateful
  workload.
- Replicas are fixed at {{ replica_count }}.
{%- endif %}
{% if replica_count > 1 or enable_hpa -%}
- **PDB** (`minAvailable: 1`) keeps a replica up across coordinated node-reboot
  drains.
{%- else -%}
- **No PDB**: at a single replica `minAvailable: 1` blocks every voluntary
  eviction, and a node could then never drain.
{%- endif %}

## Observability

{% if enable_servicemonitor -%}
- **Metrics**: the `ServiceMonitor` is discovered cluster-wide by the platform
  Prometheus, and `networkpolicy.yaml` allows the scrape from the
  `observability` namespace on the same port.
{%- else -%}
- **Metrics**: not scraped. `enable_servicemonitor` renders the ServiceMonitor
  and the matching scrape NetworkPolicy together, once the app has a real
  `/metrics`.
{%- endif %}
- **Alerts**: the `PrometheusRule` is likewise discovered cluster-wide. Both
  expressions pin `namespace` and `deployment`, because tenant rules evaluate
  across the whole cluster.
- **Reachability**: those alerts read kube-state metrics, so they cover replica
  availability, not end-to-end HTTP/TLS. A broken route, certificate or DNS
  record does not fire them while the pod stays ready — that is an operator-added
  blackbox target (ONBOARDING step O7).
- **Logs**: container stdout/stderr ships to Loki via the platform's log agent.
  Nothing to configure.

## What the operator owns vs. what you own

| Operator (cluster repo) | You (this repo) |
|---|---|
| The `Namespace` and its PSA labels | Everything under `kubernetes/flux/` |
| The `GitRepository` + `Kustomization` wiring | Workload, Service, routing CRs |
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %}
| The `ClusterSecretStore` + bootstrap secret | The `ExternalSecret` reference |
{%- endif %}
| Internal DNS records | Public routing (external-dns) |
| Platform middlewares, ClusterIssuer, VIPs | The per-host `Certificate` |
| Volume provisioning | PVC references, if any |
